# Imported from content/corn-polenta.md at da84378b36bd5b2e3cb35f610d64630bf1bd899d; retrieved 2026-10-08.
# Source metadata: - ⏲️ Prep time: 1 h
# Source metadata: - 🍽️ Servings: 4

::ingredients

(corn flour) 500g
(water, fresh) 3l plus more if needed
(salt)

::equipment

(whisk)
(spoon, big)

::instructions

{polenta} = (corn flour) + (water) 3l
    <stir, using (whisk)> ?= no lumps remain
{polenta} <heat> ?= boiling
Meanwhile [
    {polenta} <stir, slowly, using (spoon)>
]
{polenta} <cook, low heat, ~1h>
Meanwhile [
    {polenta} <stir, very often>
    If {polenta} thickens too much:
        {polenta} + (water)
]
{polenta} <season, with (salt)>

::notes

Consider that not all corns are suitable for polenta.
Polenta is not to be eaten alone; instead, it may be served in a variety of ways:
- as a side dish, often with meat
- with a side dish, like peas
- with a sauce, such as meat sauce or tomato sauce
- transformed in a main dish melting in it a slice of a savoury cheese

::story

Polenta is basically cereal flour boiled in water until thick. It was a common peasant dish in many areas of Italy, thus many versions exist, varying in consistency (from almost liquid to thick enough to make slices) and cereals used.

